  {
   "ply": 10,
   "t": 59,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/ppp1ppbp/3p1np1/8/2PPP3/2N2P2/PP4PP/R1BQKBNR w KQ - 1 6",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": "Against the King's Indian around this level the recommendation is the Sämisch, and its signature is the modest pawn to f3. The idea is one you'll have met in the Sicilian — it braces the e4-pawn like a strut, so the big centre can't be undermined.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 11,
   "t": 82.5,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/ppp1ppbp/3p1np1/8/2PPP3/2N1BP2/PP4PP/R2QKBNR b KQ - 2 6",
   "line": [
    "Be3"
   ],
   "spoken": "Resist the urge to lunge d5 too early. Instead develop the dark-squared bishop to e3 — and the deeper plan is the pawn storm with h4, prying open the kingside.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 12,
   "t": 88,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/pp2ppbp/3p1np1/2p5/2PPP3/2N1BP2/PP4PP/R2QKBNR w KQ - 0 7",
   "line": [
    "c5"
   ],
   "spoken": "The Sämisch is the one King's Indian line where the king routinely goes long. That's the mainline framework here — castle queenside and hurl the kingside pawns at the enemy king."
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 92.5,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/pp2ppbp/3p1np1/2p5/2PPP3/2N1BP2/PP2N1PP/R2QKB1R b KQ - 1 7",
   "line": [
    "Nge2"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 111,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/pp2ppbp/3p1np1/8/2PpP3/2N1BP2/PP2N1PP/R2QKB1R w KQ - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "cxd4"
   ],
   "spoken": "So f3 earns its keep two ways: it cements central control, and it slams the door on the knight jump to g4 that would otherwise harass the bishop. That bishop is precious — it isn't traded except for its opposite number.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 15,
   "t": 112.5,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/pp2ppbp/3p1np1/8/2PNP3/2N1BP2/PP4PP/R2QKB1R b KQ - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "Nxd4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 114,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/pp2ppbp/2np1np1/8/2PNP3/2N1BP2/PP4PP/R2QKB1R w KQ - 1 9",
   "line": [
    "Nc6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 17,
   "t": 132,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/pp2ppbp/2np1np1/8/2PNP3/2N1BP2/PP1Q2PP/R3KB1R b KQ - 2 9",
   "line": [
    "Qd2"
   ],
   "spoken": "The opponent has drifted into an accelerated-dragon-style setup, which isn't the best answer to the Sämisch — the position is now simply better for the first player. A quiet queen move consolidates; the tempting retreat to prevent a trade isn't needed, since the trade is perfectly fine."
  },
  {
   "ply": 18,
   "t": 154,
   "fen": "r1bqr1k1/pp2ppbp/2np1np1/8/2PNP3/2N1BP2/PP1Q2PP/R3KB1R w KQ - 3 10",
   "line": [
    "Re8"
   ],
   "spoken": "Now a word on flexibility, the mark of strong play: begin the game with one plan but be ready to stop on a dime — you lined up a layup, and now you pull up for the three. Concretely, a positional edge means you no longer have to force an immediate mate.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 19,
   "t": 172.5,
   "fen": "r1bqr1k1/pp2ppbp/2np1np1/8/2PNP3/2N1BP2/PP1Q2PP/2KR1B1R b - - 4 10",
   "line": [
    "O-O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": "That empty-looking rook move hands over the initiative, so castle long and make the intentions plain — go straight for the throat in the classical manner: h4, h5, rip open the h-file and hunt the king.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 20,
   "t": 183.5,
   "fen": "r2qr1k1/pp1bppbp/2np1np1/8/2PNP3/2N1BP2/PP1Q2PP/2KR1B1R w - - 5 11",
   "line": [
    "Bd7"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 21,
   "t": 188,
   "fen": "r2qr1k1/pp1bppbp/2np1np1/8/2PNP2P/2N1BP2/PP1Q2P1/2KR1B1R b - - 0 11",
   "line": [
    "h4"
   ],
   "spoken": "No reason to delay — push the h-pawn, fully intending to sacrifice it. Feeding that pawn is the entire point of the attack; the open lines are worth far more than the material.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 22,
   "t": 193,
   "fen": "r3r1k1/pp1bppbp/2np1np1/q7/2PNP2P/2N1BP2/PP1Q2P1/2KR1B1R w - - 1 12",
   "line": [
    "Qa5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 23,
   "t": 218.5,
   "fen": "r3r1k1/pp1bppbp/2np1np1/q7/2PNP2P/2N1BP2/PP1Q2P1/1K1R1B1R b - - 2 12",
   "line": [
    "Kb1"
   ],
   "spoken": "Here's a piece of classical prophylaxis — the king step to b1. After castling long it's a wonderfully healthy move, tucking the king off the exposed c-file so that a rook lift and queenside pressure lose much of their bite. A little insurance before the storm.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 24,
   "t": 224.5,
   "fen": "2r1r1k1/pp1bppbp/2np1np1/q7/2PNP2P/2N1BP2/PP1Q2P1/1K1R1B1R w - - 3 13",
   "line": [
    "Rac8"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
